/-!
# The relative Isom functor of two quasi-coherent modules, and its sheaf property

In the standing data of Stacks 08JT — a morphism `f : X ⟶ base` of algebraic spaces over a
scheme `S` and two quasi-coherent `𝒪_X`-modules `F`, `G` — the relative Hom functor
`Hom(F, G) : (Sch/base)ᵒᵖ ⥤ Sets` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Quot/RelativeHom.lean` has a subfunctor

`Isom(F, G)(T) = { u : F_T ⟶ G_T | u is an isomorphism of 𝒪_{X_T}-modules }`

cut out by invertibility.  It is a subfunctor because pullback of a homomorphism along a
morphism of test schemes is its inverse image read through an isomorphism of functors, and the
inverse image of an isomorphism is one.  This is the functor of Stacks 08K7, and
**Stacks 08K8** says that it satisfies the sheaf property for the fpqc topology.

The argument of the source is a reduction to the relative Hom functor.  Hom already satisfies
the sheaf property, so a compatible family of invertible homomorphisms glues to a unique
homomorphism `u : F_T ⟶ G_T`; what remains is that `u` is invertible, and that is invertibility
descending along the fpqc covering `{X_{T_i} ⟶ X_T}` of `X_T` obtained from the given covering
`{T_i ⟶ T}` by base change.  That the base changes do form an fpqc covering is **Stacks 03MR**,
part (3), and is proved below; the descent itself is the full-faithfulness clause of
Stacks 04W8, and is carried.

## Main definitions

* `CategoryTheory.Subfunctor.isSheafFor_of_local`: a subfunctor of a presheaf that is a sheaf
  for a presieve is again a sheaf for that presieve, as soon as membership of the subfunctor is
  local for the presieve.  This is the reduction of **Stacks 08K8** to the sheaf property of
  the relative Hom functor, with nothing about modules in sight.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.toModuleHom`: a section of the relative Hom functor at
  a test scheme, read as the homomorphism `F_T ⟶ G_T` of base-changed modules that it is, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.IsIsomAt`: invertibility of such a section.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.isomSubfunctor` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.isomFunctor`: **the relative Isom functor of
  Stacks 08K7**, as a subfunctor of the relative Hom functor and as a functor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ReflectsIsoAlongFpqcCovering`: the full-faithfulness clause of
  Stacks 04W8 in the form consumed here; see the implementation notes.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.Functor.isIso_conjHom_iff`: the conjugate of a morphism by a comparison of a
  functor with a composite is an isomorphism exactly when the image of the morphism under the
  further functor is.  This is what makes invertibility stable under pullback, hence
  `isomSubfunctor` a subfunctor, and it is what identifies the descent problem for `Isom` with
  the descent problem for inverse images of module homomorphisms.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.baseChangeMapIso`: the identification of the base change `X_U` of a
  test scheme with the chosen fibre product `U ×_T X_T` of Stacks 02X2.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isFpqcCovering_baseChangeMap`: **Stacks 03MR**, part (3), at the
  base changes of a covering family of test schemes: if `{U i ⟶ T}` is an fpqc covering of
  test schemes over the target then `{X_{U i} ⟶ X_T}` is an fpqc covering of `X_T`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.isIsomAt_of_isFpqcCovering`: the point the source's
  proof isolates — a homomorphism `F_T ⟶ G_T` whose base changes along an fpqc covering of
  test schemes are isomorphisms is an isomorphism.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.isSheafFor_isomFunctor`: **Stacks 08K8**, the sheaf
  property of the relative Isom functor for an fpqc covering of test schemes over the target.

## Implementation notes

**The sheaf property is stated for a covering family.**  There is no fpqc topology on the test
schemes over an algebraic space in this development, so "satisfies the sheaf property for the
fpqc topology" is read as `CategoryTheory.Presieve.IsSheafFor` for the presieve generated by an
arbitrary fpqc covering family of test schemes, which is the elementary meaning of the phrase
and the form in which the source's proof is written.  The covering hypothesis is the fpqc
condition of Stacks 03MQ for the underlying family of algebraic spaces,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsFpqcCovering`, rather than for the family of schemes; the two agree,
a covering of schemes being a covering of the algebraic spaces they represent.

**Two inputs are carried, and neither is the conclusion.**

The first is the sheaf property of the relative Hom functor itself, which is Stacks 08JV.  It
is a statement about `Hom(F, G)`, not about `Isom(F, G)`: it supplies the glued homomorphism
and the uniqueness, and says nothing about invertibility.  Stacks 08JV is not available in this
development.

The second is the full-faithfulness clause of Stacks 04W8 — fpqc descent of quasi-coherent
modules on algebraic spaces — isolated as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ReflectsIsoAlongFpqcCovering`: along an fpqc covering of algebraic
spaces, a homomorphism of quasi-coherent modules whose inverse images are isomorphisms is an
isomorphism.  It is a statement about one morphism of modules on one algebraic space, with no
test schemes, no relative Hom functor and no sheaf condition in it.  Stacks 04W8 is not
available in this development either.

What is *not* carried is the step between them, which is where the source's proof does its
work: the covering of `X_T` that the descent is applied to has to be produced from the given
covering of `T`, and that is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isFpqcCovering_baseChangeMap` below,
Stacks 03MR part (3) pulled back along `X_T ⟶ T` and transported along the pasted-pullback
identification `X_{U i} ≅ X_T ×_T U i` of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isPullback_baseChangeMap`.  Nor is the reduction of the sheaf property
of a subfunctor to that of its ambient presheaf carried; that is
`CategoryTheory.Subfunctor.isSheafFor_of_local`.

**Quasi-coherence of the base-changed modules is a hypothesis**, exactly as it is for
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.baseChangedSituation`, the descent clause of Stacks 04W8
being about quasi-coherent modules.  It is a hypothesis of these statements only because it is
a *field* of `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation`: the fact itself is Stacks 03GA, proved
along an arbitrary morphism of algebraic spaces by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_modulePullback_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ModulePullbackQuasicoherentFromChartDiagrams.lean`, so a
consumer holding quasi-coherence of the two modules on `X` can always fill the fields in.  The
hypothesis is recorded in the tag attribute.

**Invertibility is a predicate on the Hom set, not a second Hom set.**
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.homAt` is a `def` rather than an `abbrev`, for the
unifier reasons set out in `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Quot/RelativeHom.lean`, so a section
of the relative Hom functor is read as a homomorphism of the base-changed modules through the
accessor `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.toModuleHom`, which is the identity.  Every
statement about invertibility below is phrased with that accessor rather than with an inline
ascription, and the one computation with content,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HomSituation.isIsomAt_homMap_iff`, is reduced to
`CategoryTheory.Functor.isIso_conjHom_iff` at an explicitly given comparison: stated inline
instead, the unifier unfolds the Hom set to the underlying homomorphisms of sheaves of modules
and does not terminate within the heartbeat limit, which is the same obstruction the relative
Hom functor's own functoriality laws work around.

